DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The kkvvmm__ddppccppuu__sseettccppuu(), kkvvmm__ggeettmmaaxxccppuu(), and kkvvmm__ggeettppccppuu() functions are
     used to access the per-CPU data of active processors in the kernel indi‐
     cated by _k_d.  Per-CPU storage comes in two flavours: data stored directly
     in a _s_t_r_u_c_t _p_c_p_u associated with each CPU, and dynamic per-CPU storage
     (DPCPU), in which a single kernel symbol refers to different data depend‐
     ing on what CPU it is accessed from.

     The kkvvmm__ggeettmmaaxxccppuu() function returns the maximum number of CPUs supported
     by the kernel.

     The kkvvmm__ggeettnnccppuuss() function returns the current number of CPUs in the
     kernel.

     The kkvvmm__ggeettppccppuu() function returns a buffer holding the per-CPU data for
     a single CPU.  This buffer is described by the _s_t_r_u_c_t _p_c_p_u type.  The
     caller is responsible for releasing the buffer via a call to free(3) when
     it is no longer needed.  If _c_p_u is not active, then NULL is returned in‐
     stead.

     The kkvvmm__rreeaadd__zzppccppuu() function is used to obtain private per-CPU copy from
     a UMA_ZONE_PCPU zone(9).  It takes _b_a_s_e argument as base address of an
     allocation and copyies _s_i_z_e bytes into _b_u_f from the part of allocation
     that is private to _c_p_u.

     The kkvvmm__ccoouunntteerr__uu6644__ffeettcchh() function fetches value of a counter(9)
     pointed by _b_a_s_e address.

     Symbols for dynamic per-CPU data are accessed via kvm_nlist(3) as with
     other symbols.  lliibbkkvvmm maintains a notion of the "current CPU", set by
     kkvvmm__ddppccppuu__sseettccppuu(), which defaults to 0.  Once another CPU is selected,
     kvm_nlist(3) will return pointers to that data on the appropriate CPU.

CCAACCHHIINNGG
     kkvvmm__ggeettmmaaxxccppuu() and kkvvmm__ggeettppccppuu() cache the nlist values for various ker‐
     nel variables which are reused in successive calls.  You may call either
     function with _k_d set to NULL to clear this cache.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     On success, the kkvvmm__ggeettmmaaxxccppuu() function returns the maximum number of
     CPUs supported by the kernel.  If an error occurs, it returns -1 instead.

     On success, the kkvvmm__ggeettppccppuu() function returns a pointer to an allocated
     buffer or NULL.  If an error occurs, it returns -1 instead.

     On success, the kkvvmm__ddppccppuu__sseettccppuu() call returns 0; if an error occurs, it
     returns -1 instead.

     On success, the kkvvmm__rreeaadd__zzppccppuu() function returns number of bytes copied.
     If an error occurs, it returns -1 instead.

     If any function encounters an error, then an error message may be re‐
     trieved via kvm_geterr(3).

